WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.260
Source control is the first CI/CD control point.

2
00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:20.280
Protected branches, pull requests, required reviews, code-owner review, protected tags, release branches, merge rules, and signed commits or tags where policy requires them all help define which changes can become release-bound software.

3
00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:24.940
The controls should match the importance of the branch and artifact being produced.

4
00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:30.880
A prototype branch does not need the same treatment as a release branch, but the difference should be explicit.

5
00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:35.800
Pipeline files deserve the same seriousness as production source code.

6
00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:48.540
A change to a workflow, build script, dependency manifest, packaging step, signing step, or deployment rule can change what gets compiled, tested, signed, published, or deployed.

7
00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:52.480
Treating these files as ordinary maintenance can create hidden release risk.

8
00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:59.060
Reviewers should understand not only the syntax change, but also the trust change it introduces.

9
00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:00.940
Trigger rules also matter.

10
00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:09.600
Teams should know which branches, tags, pull requests, schedules, manual approvals, or release events start jobs.

11
00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:13.220
A pull-request build may need a different trust model than a release build.

12
00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:19.960
Experimental branches should not casually reach production credentials, signing jobs, or release repositories.

13
00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:27.420
Documenting trigger intent helps reviewers recognize when a workflow change expands the release path.

14
00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:30.580
Good change gates make intent explicit.

15
00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:39.640
They separate exploratory work from release-bound work, require review for sensitive workflow changes, preserve approval records, and make release promotion traceable.

16
00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:42.620
The result is not bureaucracy for its own sake.

17
00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:49.360
It is a control surface that helps teams explain why a build was trusted and who approved the path from change to release.

18
00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:57.460
When pressure rises, those records keep the release conversation grounded in evidence instead of memory and assumption during release review and approval.
